pdf: buffered fast paths in the content-stream lexer

With fz_read_byte inlinable (0040) the lexer's remaining cost is structural:
a read (and often an unread) per byte, and tokens copied into the scratch
buffer byte by byte. Content streams are mostly whitespace, integers and
short operator names, so:

- lex_white skips whitespace directly in the stream buffer and only falls
  back to the byte loop when it reaches the buffer end.
- lex_number, for a run of digits terminated by whitespace or a delimiter
  that is already buffered, accumulates the value while scanning (the same
  wrapping uint64 arithmetic as fast_atoi) and returns PDF_TOK_INT.
- lex_name, for a run of regular bytes with no '#' escape ending in buffered
  whitespace or a delimiter, memcpy's it into the scratch in one go.

Signs, '.', '#', a run touching the buffer end or the scratch limit, or any
other terminator take the unchanged general paths, so tokens are identical.
---
diff --git a/source/pdf/pdf-lex.c b/source/pdf/pdf-lex.c
index aaee6aff8..2ca4d23b5 100644
--- a/source/pdf/pdf-lex.c
+++ b/source/pdf/pdf-lex.c
@@ -80,6 +80,17 @@ static inline int iswhite(int ch)
 		ch == '\040';
 }
 
+/* the IS_DELIM set as a predicate */
+static inline int isdelim(int ch)
+{
+	return
+		ch == '(' || ch == ')' ||
+		ch == '<' || ch == '>' ||
+		ch == '[' || ch == ']' ||
+		ch == '{' || ch == '}' ||
+		ch == '/' || ch == '%';
